    story += [p("6. Daily prediction comparisons","H1x"),p("The plots below show representative test predictions at horizons 1, 7 and 30 for the selected overall model. Day-one estimates respond most closely to recent bridge conditions. At horizon 30, predictions are smoother and extreme peaks are harder to reproduce because the origin contains no intervening observed traffic.")]+fig(f/"predictions_by_horizon.png","Figure 3. Actual and selected-model forecasts at horizons 1, 7 and 30.",6.8*inch,5.55*inch)+[PageBreak()]
    story += [p("7. Weekly and 30-day aggregate performance","H1x"),p("Daily predictions were summed within each forecast origin and compared with the corresponding observed 7-day or 30-day total. Aggregation can cancel some positive and negative daily errors, but systematic bias remains visible."),table([["Target","Model","Period","Aggregate MAE","MAPE","R²"]]+[[x.target,x.model,f"{int(x.period_days)} days",f"{x.MAE:,.0f}",f"{x.MAPE:.2f}%",f"{x.R2:.3f}"] for _,x in agg.sort_values(["target","period_days","MAE"]).iterrows()],[.75*inch,1.05*inch,.75*inch,1.2*inch,.75*inch,.65*inch],6.8),p("Weekly totals are suited to staff and lane planning, while 30-day totals can support budgeting. They should not be interpreted as 30 independent one-day forecasts: all predictions in a row share the same origin information."),PageBreak()]
    ev=m[m.horizon_band.isin(["holiday","Eid +/-7d"])].sort_values(["target","horizon_band","MAE"]);story += [p("8. Holiday and Eid performance","H1x"),table([["Target","Segment","Model","N","MAE","MAPE","R²"]]+[[x.target,x.horizon_band,x.model,str(int(x.N)),f"{x.MAE:,.0f}",f"{x.MAPE:.2f}%",f"{x.R2:.3f}"] for _,x in ev.iterrows()],[.65*inch,.85*inch,1.0*inch,.55*inch,1.0*inch,.7*inch,.55*inch],6.2),p("Eid errors are much higher than overall errors. The test set contains relatively few distinct Eid events even though each event creates many origin–horizon rows. Those rows are correlated and should not be mistaken for thousands of independent festivals. Event-specific modeling and uncertainty intervals are therefore important next steps."),PageBreak()]
    story += [p("9. Prediction uncertainty and operational use","H1x"),p("The current deliverable produces point predictions. Longer horizons require wider uncertainty bands because weather, traffic composition and unexpected disruptions are not fully known. A future version should add quantile boosting or split-conformal intervals evaluated separately by horizon."),table([["Mode","Recommended interpretation"],["Next day","Operational traffic/toll estimate using fresh origin history"],["Next 7 days","Daily schedule plus weekly total; use forecast weather"],["Next 30 days","Planning scenario; substitute climatology where weather is unavailable"],["Eid period","High-uncertainty scenario requiring event monitoring"]],[1.25*inch,5.25*inch]),p("10. Limitations","H1x")]+bullets(["Approximately four years of history limit the number of repeated annual events.","Historical observed weather is more accurate than real long-range weather forecasts.","Future Jamuna observations are intentionally excluded after the origin.","Accidents, closures, strikes and toll policy changes are not modeled.","The compact local training configurations are reproducible but not an exhaustive hyperparameter search.","Point forecasts do not yet include calibrated prediction intervals."])+[PageBreak()]
    story += [p("11. Conclusion","H1x"),p("The multi-horizon extension successfully trains a single horizon-aware forecasting system for daily traffic and toll revenue from one to thirty days ahead. XGBoost is the strongest overall traffic model and CatBoost is the strongest overall toll model on test MAE. The results demonstrate useful planning ability while quantifying the expected loss of accuracy as the horizon grows. The most important deployment requirements are origin-safe feature updates, realistic future-weather inputs and explicit uncertainty for Eid and 30-day forecasts."),p("Appendix A. Delivered artifacts","H1x"),p("Eight serialized models, 6,255 row-level test predictions, 72 horizon/event metric rows, weekly and monthly aggregate tables, figures, a run summary and the complete training script are included."),p("Appendix B. Reproduction","H1x"),p("From the multi-horizon project folder, run: <font name='Courier'>python train_multihorizon.py</font>. It regenerates the expanded samples, saved models, tables and figures."),p("References","H1x"),p("1. Bangladesh Bridge Authority, Padma Bridge traffic and toll records, https://bba.gov.bd/<br/>2. Open-Meteo historical weather documentation, https://open-meteo.com/en/docs/historical-weather-api<br/>3. CSE 4112 Group B2 multi-horizon forecasting brief, September 2026.","Small")]
